### What we have completed inside WSL (Ubuntu 24.04)

1. **Enabled systemd**

   ```bash
   sudo tee /etc/wsl.conf <<'EOF'
   [boot]
   systemd=true
   EOF
   ```

   Then from Windows:

   ```powershell
   wsl --shutdown
   wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04
   ```

2. **Installed open-source Docker Engine**

   ```bash
   sudo apt-get update
   sudo apt-get install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg lsb-release
   sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
   curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | \
        sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
   echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg] \
        https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable" | \
        sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list
   sudo apt-get update
   sudo apt-get install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io \
        docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin
   sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
   newgrp docker
   sudo systemctl enable --now docker
   docker run --rm hello-world    # verified daemon works
   ```

3. **Created private bridge network**

   ```bash
   docker network create llm-net
   ```

4. **Set Windows startup task to wake WSL automatically**
   *(run in elevated Windows PowerShell)*

   ```powershell
   schtasks /Create /SC ONSTART /RU SYSTEM /TN WakeWSL ^
            /TR "wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 --exec /bin/true"
   ```

   This guarantees Docker (and any restart-enabled containers) start right after every reboot.

